  H3 Refreshment or feeding of the starter
  [link] 
  figcaption: Recently refreshed sourdough
  p: As it ferments, sometimes for several days, the volume of the starter is increased by periodic additions of flour and water, called refreshments or referred to as a feeding. As long as this starter culture is fed flour a
    [link] ratio
  p: A starter prepared from scratch with a salted wheat-rye dough takes about 54 hours at 27 °C (81 °F) to stabilise at a pH between 4.4 and 4.6. 4% salt inhibits L. sanfranciscensis, while C. milleri can withstand 8%.
    [link] acetic acid
    [link] Desem
  H3 Intervals between refreshments
    [link] F. sanfranciscensis
  p: The intervals between refreshments of the starter may be reduced in order to increase the rate of gas (CO2) production, a process described as "acceleration." In this process, the ratio of yeasts to lactobacilli may be a
    [link] petri dishes
  p: Maintaining metabolically active sourdough with high leavening activity typically requires several refreshments per day, which is achieved in bakeries that use sourdough as sole leavening agents but not by amateur bakers
